+   <PaletteStyleTag> inserts a `#sidebar-palette` <style> tag at runtime that
+   targets `.rcx-sidebar--main, .rcx-sidepanel, .rcx-navbar` with its own
+   forced-dark surface-sidebar value, and it lands AFTER this bundled
+   main.css in the DOM — exactly the risk flagged (but unverified) in the
+   original comment above. On tied specificity (single class selector, same
+   as ours), source order wins and the dynamic tag beat us, so the sidebar
+   rendered as Fuselage's dark-mode gray (#2f343d) instead of our navy.
+   Fixed by doubling the selector to raise specificity above the dynamic
+   tag's, per this project's own CSS rule (escalate specificity before
+   `!important`) — no need to touch every other token this way, only the
+   ones actually being contested here. */
+.rcx-sidebar--main.rcx-sidebar--main,
+.rcx-sidepanel.rcx-sidepanel {
 	--rcx-color-surface-sidebar: #0f1728;
 }
 
-.rcx-navbar {
+.rcx-navbar.rcx-navbar {
 	--rcx-color-surface-sidebar: #0a2535;
 }
